  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \ncircRMST knockdown in SHP77 NEPC/SCLC cells induced transcriptional changes with 6,382 common DEGs (shRNA1/shRNA2). circRMST depletion reduced tumor growth in vivo and caused cell-cycle arrest, consistent with regulatory roles in proliferation and neuroendocrine identity. Dataset: Teng et al., Cancer Cell 2025 (SHP77 RNA-seq).  \n\n**Keywords**  \ncircRMST, neuroendocrine prostate cancer (NEPC), SHP77, ASCL1, cell-cycle arrest, GSEA, ribosome, stress response.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \nASCL1, CD24, GRP, SESN3, CYP1A1, AKR1C1, PTP4A1, ITGB1, KCNE4, PDE1C.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n*No direct GSEA results provided; inferred from DEGs and known gene functions:*  \n- **Upregulated**: Ribosome biogenesis (Metazoa_SRP), stress response (SESN3, CYP1A1), detoxification (AKR1C1), integrin signaling (ITGB1).  \n- **Downregulated**: Neuroendocrine markers (ASCL1, GRP), cell-cycle regulators (CD24, PTBP3, EIF3I), lipid metabolism (DHRS2).  \n\n**Targeted Subset**  \nASCL1 (downregulated, log2FC -3.07/-2.24) is a master regulator of NEPC differentiation and tumor growth. GRP (downregulated, log2FC -3.01/-2.55) drives neuroendocrine signaling. SESN3 (upregulated, log2FC 2.51/2.31) links to stress-induced cell-cycle control.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins**  \n**Upregulated**:  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                  | Pathway(s)                |  \n|------------|-----------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| **SESN3**  | Up        | Stress response, DNA damage    | p53 signaling, ROS        |  \n| **CYP1A1** | Up        | Xenobiotic metabolism          | Aryl hydrocarbon receptor |  \n| **ITGB1**  | Up        | Cell adhesion, migration       | Integrin signaling        |  \n\n**Downregulated**:  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                  | Pathway(s)                |  \n|------------|-----------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| **ASCL1**  | Down      | Neuroendocrine lineage driver  | NEPC differentiation      |  \n| **CD24**   | Down      | Metastasis, stemness           | P-selectin binding        |  \n| **GRP**    | Down      | Autocrine growth factor        | Bombesin receptor         |  \n\n**Implications**  \nASCL1 downregulation aligns with impaired NEPC growth and cell-cycle arrest. SESN3/CYP1A1 induction suggests compensatory stress responses. Targeting ASCL1 or upstream regulators (e.g., circRMST) could disrupt NEPC maintenance.  \n\n**Next Steps**  \n1. Validate ASCL1 downregulation in NEPC models post-circRMST knockdown (e.g., qPCR, IHC).  \n2. Perform GSEA using the ranked DEG list to confirm enrichment of neuroendocrine and cell-cycle pathways.  \n3. Test ASCL1 overexpression to rescue circRMST knockdown phenotypes.  \n4. Investigate circRMST-ASCL1 mechanistic linkage (e.g., RNA-binding protein interactions).  \n\n**Rationale**  \nASCL1 is a NEPC-specific oncogene not observed in non-neuroendocrine prostate cancer. Its downregulation here directly links circRMST to NEPC pathogenicity. Stress response upregulation (SESN3/CYP1A1) may reflect adaptive resistance mechanisms.",
